A||N||19||stimpy||A/C or S/C...hmmmm||||1||
Z||000000||stimpy||07-24-2000||10:54 PM||||Well, today was the day my AC compressor retired from service. Not with a whimper but with a big freakin screaching noise and plumes of acrid black smoke from the belt! Sh!t!!!! I have had quotes of up to $1G to get it fixed. Question... at this point, am I better off just putting in the supercharger and drive really fast with the windows down?  But really, do I have to replace ALL of the AC components or is it possible to just replace the compressor?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Cooter||07-24-2000||11:10 PM||||Flush the system with a quality A/C solvent. Replace the compressor, receiver, orifice tube and all O rings. I did mine on my 88gt and the air works great. With temps around here at 100 degrees, I would much rather have A/C and drive slower!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Standard||07-25-2000||06:10 AM||||Supercharger Supercharger Supercharger!! you <B>know</B> you want to do it... hehe.<P>------------------<BR>Mike -- Golden Valley, MN<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 147,000 miles and counting!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000003||stimpy||07-25-2000||07:36 AM||||In Minnesota it would be an easy decision. In Oklahoma where we've been having 100 degree highs and 80 degree dewpoint I gotta have that a/c.<P>Cooter- sounds a lot like you did your own work on your a/c. Am I correct?  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||07-25-2000||09:48 AM||||If you are going to keep the A/C, I would get the new compressor and convert to R134 at the same time. (Personally I would seriously think about the supercharger first. My car isn't a daily driver it's a weekend driver)<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||deceler8||07-25-2000||10:21 AM||||If you can do the work yourself, you should be able to restore your A/C and convert to R134 for $300 or so.<P>And it's entirely possible your car will have a higher resale value with working air vs. no air and a supercharger. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||pollock||07-25-2000||04:02 PM||||Stimp, it's too damn hot here.  Fix the AC, and put a nice shiny blue bottle in the trunk for a fraction of the cost of the SC.<P>And then kick ass.||||reg||1
Z||000007||stsmithgt||07-25-2000||05:22 PM||||The lowest I've seen a compressor for is about 250.00 bucks.  Well that was AFTER the garage charged me about 800.00 plus labor to put one in my car.(bastards) It took 5 visits for them to get it right too.(double bastards)AND it's still R-12.  They saw me coming.  The compressor was one of the last things they replaced so they nickel and dimed me to death before a final total of about 1300.00 or so.  I make bad advertizing for WAYSIDE GARAGE, SANDCITY CALIFORNIA MONTEREY COUNTY.  I gave them a second chance(why?) to fix my front manifold. GOD talk about having to hold their hand through the whole procedure.  I wish I had the time to do it myself.  Oops getting off the subject. later. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000008||EdsB52||07-25-2000||07:12 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cooter:<BR><B>Flush the system with a quality A/C solvent. Replace the compressor, receiver, orifice tube and all O rings. I did mine on my 88gt and the air works great. With temps around here at 100 degrees, I would much rather have A/C and drive slower!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>100 degrees? You don't need A/C at a frosty 100 degrees! Ed from Phoenix!<BR>||||reg||8
Z||000009||stimpy||07-25-2000||08:13 PM||||But it's a dry heat.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Pollock... no jive! I damn near melted on the way home tonight.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://gifs123.tripod.com/page15/renstimpy2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||fierospeeder||07-25-2000||08:31 PM||||I was wondering on how many orings/seals there are?  Is it at every single fitting?  My moms compressor is leaking oil, and i want to change it plus convert it to 134.  Its the same 2.8 engine.  I seen new compressors on wrenchhead.com for less then 200 bucks.  I want to fix up the car before i sell it.  thanks ||||reg||1
Z||000011||JimIndyRCR||07-25-2000||10:22 PM||||Ed from Phoenix is right- 100 degrees is cold. I just restored and delivered an 85 GT 5 Speed from LA to Dallas, about 10 miles outside of Phoenix @ 11pm the A/C belt melted so no A/C and it was 112 DEGREES!! a commercial size belt fixed the problem. Something else to consider...engine heat will come forward on the freeway w/o the A/C working. The fix is easy, stay with r-12.||||reg||1
Z||000012||stimpy||07-25-2000||10:35 PM||||The fix is <B>how easy</B>?  I'd like to explore other opportuniies besides throwing hideous gobs of money at it!<BR>Is this something in the ability of a shadetree mechanic? I appreciate all responses, some guidance from someone who has been down this road would be great!||||reg||1
Z||000013||EdsB52||07-26-2000||12:43 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JimIndyRCR:<BR><B>Ed from Phoenix is right- 100 degrees is cold. I just restored and delivered an 85 GT 5 Speed from LA to Dallas, about 10 miles outside of Phoenix @ 11pm the A/C belt melted so no A/C and it was 112 DEGREES!! a commercial size belt fixed the problem. Something else to consider...engine heat will come forward on the freeway w/o the A/C working. The fix is easy, stay with r-12.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Right on Bro. I just installed an A/C compressor, lines, and basically everything except the condensor and evaporator on my 89 Ford P/U. The compressor came without oil and gave me the option of 134a or R12, I went with the very expensive R12 at the dismay of everyone who contended that 134 would appreciate the value of the vehicle. WHATEVER!!! Even the R12 works marginally at 3 in the afternoon! Later JimIndyRCR.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||JimIndyRCR||07-26-2000||11:14 AM||||Stimpy,<BR>Cooter says it all. I would first disconnect the drier and remove the filter [orifice tube] (so access to the lines will be easier) then disconnect the hose coupling at the rear of car (under the air intake tube) and remove the compressor with the hoses attached- Car has to be high in the air and compressor comes out the bottom. With everything disassembled flush all the lines with high quality A/C flush-an air compressor will be needed to do a thorough job here. Next buy a new compressor-or if buying rebuilt- buy close to your home as rebuilds tend to fail under warranty. Finally, replace all oil seals you removed (with new ones). Then you get to have someone (shop?) evacuate and recharge the system. Yes R-12 is expensive but worth it!!! Shop around for the best $$ on this as shops will vary, alot.<BR>Jim||||reg||1
Z||000015||stimpy||07-26-2000||07:35 PM||||Cool, thanks for the response Jim. Fortunately one of our club members has his own well stocked shop at his house. Another question though. I saw on carparts.com that they sell the ac clutch assembly seperately. Is it possible to just replace the ac clutch without having to purge and recharge the system? Cuz the ac was still blowing cold when the clutch seemed to go out. ||||reg||1
Z||000016||JimIndyRCR||07-26-2000||07:42 PM||||Stimpy,<BR>R you sure it's only the clutch and not the compressor itself? If it's the clutch-yes you can replace the clutch only, however in a fiero it's not easy- (assumimg you're leaving the compressor in the car so you don't have to evacuate and recharge the system.)<BR>Jim||||reg||1
Z||000017||stimpy||07-26-2000||07:49 PM||||Noooo, I'm not sure. Is there a way to accurately diagnose it? It would be a waste to buy a new clutch and then have to buy a new compressor assembly anyway. But it sure would be nice to be able to just replace the clutch.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Phil||07-31-2000||06:03 PM||||My son's A/C comp did the same thing screech puff of smoke and broken belt. Definately the comp. cuz I tried to turn it by hand and nada. Anyway if the clutch failed it would just spin free. Pep Boys guarntees the comp if you change the dryer/acc too. Like someone said about $300 to do the whole thing||||reg||1
Z||000019||Greg Piet||07-31-2000||06:41 PM||||OK I am confused.<BR>looking up compressors for my 2.8<BR>in the pontiac manual it says I have a da 6 compressor.<P>checker auto online has 4 different compressors<BR>two are da6<BR>two are hr6<P>then there are two size pulleys 5.7" and 4.9" for each type of compressor<P>I want to use R134, so which compressor do I want???? <P>I am getting sick of driving around with the cabin temperature at 120+ with the windows open and the vista vent out!||||reg||1
